// The attribution case that cannot be faked: a test that PANICS, followed by another test in the
// SAME file.
//
// `panic` is uncatchable (specs/safety.md), so the first test takes the whole process down. The
// binary cannot report either outcome — the panicking test never writes an end marker, and the
// second test never writes a begin marker at all. The only reading of that stream is "crashed" and
// "did not run"; what the second must never be read as is a PASS, since nothing observed it.
//
// The runner then re-queues the unobserved test into a fresh process, so this one is finally
// reported by whatever it really does there — a pass, here. The two steps are the point: absence is
// classified as absence FIRST, and only an actual run can turn it into a verdict. Disable the
// re-queue and this test reports DID NOT RUN, which is how that ordering was checked.

test 'panics on purpose'
	panic("this test takes the process down")
end 'panics on purpose'

test 'runs after the panicking test'
	print("this line must not be reached\n")
end 'runs after the panicking test'
